<manifest version="1.0" type="device" target-level="2">
<!--
  VINTF device manifest for OPPO A5s (CPH1909, MT6765).

  This is the stock ColorOS 5.2 (Android 8.1) /vendor/manifest.xml: the
  authoritative list of what the prebuilt vendor blobs actually register, plus
  the three HALs LineageOS 17.1 builds because Android 10 requires them and an
  8.1 vendor image never had them (health@2.0, usb@1.0, drm@1.2 clearkey).

  This is a legacy-Treble device. It launched with Android 8.1 / API 27, which
  is FCM (target) level 2, O-MR1. Android 10 replaced the old
  PRODUCT_COMPATIBILITY_MATRIX_LEVEL_OVERRIDE with the `target-level` attribute
  on this manifest, so level 2 is declared explicitly. Without the attribute,
  assemble_vintf would infer the same level from
  PRODUCT_SHIPPING_API_LEVEL=27 and print a warning. With it, the Q framework
  checks these HALs against the frozen O-MR1 framework compatibility matrix;
  all ten required HALs of that matrix are present here.

  The stock 8.1 compatibility_matrix.xml (the vendor->framework device matrix)
  is deliberately not shipped: it was written for the 8.1 framework and would
  reject Android 10.

  <sepolicy> is not decorative: with PRODUCT_SHIPPING_API_LEVEL=27 the build
  enables PRODUCT_ENFORCE_VINTF_MANIFEST and assemble_vintf checks this
  manifest against the FCM 2 matrix. Leaving the version unset makes it default
  to 0.0, which fails that check. 27.0 is both the version the stock 8.1 vendor
  image was built against and one of the platform's
  PLATFORM_SEPOLICY_COMPAT_VERSIONS.
-->
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.audio</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <fqname>@2.0::IDevicesFactory/default</fqname>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.audio.effect</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <fqname>@2.0::IEffectsFactory/default</fqname>
